      ],
      "command_options": [
        {
          "name": "tab",
          "flags": "--tab <targetId>",
          "help": "Target tab/page identity returned by \"browser open\", \"browser tab new\", or \"browser tab list\"",
          "takes_value": "required"
        },
        {
          "name": "timeout",
          "flags": "--timeout <ms>",
          "help": "Timeout in milliseconds",
          "takes_value": "required",
          "default": "10000"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "namespace_options": [
    {
      "name": "window",
      "flags": "--window <mode>",
      "help": "Browser window mode: foreground or background",
      "takes_value": "required"
    }
  ],
  "global_options": [
    {
      "name": "version",
      "flags": "-V, --version",
      "help": "output the version number"
    },
    {
      "name": "profile",
      "flags": "--profile <name>",
      "help": "Chrome profile/context alias for Browser Bridge commands",
      "takes_value": "required"
    }
  ],
  "structured_help": {
    "formats": [
      "yaml",
      "json"
    ],
    "usage": "opencli browser --help -f yaml"
  }
}

<session> is a required positional: pass the name of the browser session every subcommand should operate on. Reuse the same name across calls to keep the tab/state alive; pick a different name to isolate parallel browser work.

Examples:
  $ opencli browser work open https://x.com
  $ opencli browser work open https://x.com --window background
  $ opencli browser work click 12
  $ opencli browser work state
  $ opencli browser work bind
  $ opencli browser work unbind

